## Definition --> Terraform 

- Terraform is an Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool developed by HashiCorp. 
- It allows developers to define, create, manage, and delete cloud infrastructure using configuration files instead of doing everything manually.

- It is used to provision, configure, manage, and delete infrastructure such as AWS EC2 instances, S3 buckets, VPCs, databases, and security groups using code.

- Terraform uses declarative configuration files to define the desired infrastructure. 

- It compares the current infrastructure with the desired configuration and determines what changes need to be made.

- In simple words: Terraform allows us to create and manage cloud infrastructure automatically using code instead of doing it manually through the cloud console.

- Example: We can write Terraform code to create an AWS EC2 instance, security group, and other required resources, and Terraform will create them automatically.

Purpose
Automates infrastructure provisioning.
Makes infrastructure repeatable and consistent.
Helps manage cloud resources such as EC2, S3, VPC, databases, and security groups.
Reduces manual work and configuration errors.
Functionality

Terraform uses declarative configuration files to define the desired infrastructure. It then compares the desired configuration with the current infrastructure and determines what changes are required.

Main commands:

terraform init      → Initialize the project
terraform plan      → Preview changes
terraform apply     → Create/update infrastructure
terraform destroy   → Delete infrastructure

In simple words:
Terraform allows us to define infrastructure as code and automatically create, manage, update, and delete cloud resources.
